.TH TKLAYOUT 2
.SH NAME
tklayout \- find Tk widgets that did not get the room they asked for
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "tk.m";
include "tklayout.m";
tklayout := load Tklayout Tklayout->PATH;

Clipped, Overlap: con iota;

Problem: adt {
    kind:  int;
    a:     string;
    b:     string;
    ra:    Draw->Rect;
    rb:    Draw->Rect;

    text:  fn(p: self ref Problem): string;
};

init:      fn();
children:  fn(top: ref Tk->Toplevel, path: string): list of string;
walk:      fn(top: ref Tk->Toplevel, path: string): list of string;
check:     fn(top: ref Tk->Toplevel, root: string): list of ref Problem;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tklayout
reports widgets whose layout does not fit what they hold.
It exists for one failure in particular: a window whose pixel geometry was
counted out around one font, shown in another.
.I Libtk
sizes each widget from its own font \(em see
.IR tkofftest (1)
\(em so the widgets are right and the window around them is not.
.PP
.B Init
must be called before anything else.
.TP
.BI children: "top path"
The paths one level below
.IR path ,
in the order the widgets were created, or nil.
.TP
.BI walk: "top path"
Every descendant of
.IR path ,
parents before children.
.TP
.BI check: "top root"
Everything wrong under
.IR root ,
as a list of
.BR Problem s.
.B Problem.text
renders one for a person.
Two kinds are reported:
.RS
.TP
.B Clipped
The widget asked for more room than it was given, so what it holds is cut
off.
.B Ra
is what it got and
.B rb
what it needed.
.TP
.B Overlap
Two widgets laid out inside the same master occupy some of the same
pixels.
.RE
.SS Why it measures what it does
Three ways to see a broken layout were tried here and only the third
works.
.PP
Overlapping siblings is the obvious one and very nearly cannot happen:
this
.B Tk
has only
.B pack
and
.BR grid ,
no
.BR place ,
and both hand out disjoint space.
The check is kept, because a canvas window could still do it, but it has
not been seen to fire.
.PP
A widget outside the pane cannot happen at all.
.B Tk
clips a widget to the room there is, so a label needing 502 pixels in a
200-pixel window reports itself as 200 wide.
A checker built on that would have passed everything, always, which is
worth knowing before writing one.
.PP
What does work is the room asked for against the room given.
.B Tk->Required
is not clipped \(em the same label reports 502 \(em so the difference
between the two is exactly the text that was cut off, per widget, by
name.
.SS What counts as a widget
Only widgets a geometry manager has.
One that was created and never packed still reports a rectangle, its own
border, and programs here create such widgets deliberately:
.B appl/wm/toolbar.b
makes a
.B "button .b -text {XXX}"
solely to ask how tall a button would be, and never packs it.
.PP
Widgets are grouped by the master they are laid out inside, which is not
always their parent by path.
.B appl/wm/about.b
does
.BR "pack .b .l1 -in .f" ,
leaving both named under the root and laying them out inside
.BR .f ;
grouped by name, the frame appears to overlap its own contents.
.PP
This uses three
.B winfo
queries added for it \(em
.BR children ,
.B manager
and
.B master
\(em and degrades to reporting nothing against a
.B libtk
without them.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tklayout.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tk (2),
.IR tkclient (2),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.B /doc/ui-modernization-plan.md
.SH BUGS
It sees widgets, not drawing.
A program that renders its own text into an image \(em which the games and
the
.I temple
ports do \(em is invisible to it.
.PP
A window clamped by the screen rather than by its own geometry reports the
same way, so on a small display a report may mean only that the window
would not fit.
.PP
Nothing is checked about text inside a
.B text
or
.B entry
widget, whose size is given in characters and rows and so does not follow
the font by design.
